Source: djinni
Section: libdevel
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Robert J. Hansen <rjh@sixdemonbag.org>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               meson (>= 1.0.0),
               ninja-build,
               g++ (>= 14),
               pkg-config
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/rjhansen/djinni
Rules-Requires-Root: no

# This is deliberately Architecture: any rather than Architecture: all.
# The package content (headers) is architecture-independent, but its
# pkg-config file installs into the multiarch-triplet library directory
# (e.g. /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pkgconfig/djinni.pc), and an
# Architecture: all package installing into an arch-specific path is a
# well-known source of packaging problems. Building one binary package per
# architecture avoids that entirely, at the cost of a few redundant builds
# of identical content.
Package: libdjinni-dev
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: header-only C++23 library implementing Ohlmann-Thomas compressed annealing
 Djinni is a header-only C++23 library implementing Ohlmann-Thomas
 compressed annealing, a simulated-annealing variant originally developed
 for the Traveling Salesman Problem with Time Windows. It ships as a small
 set of headers with no runtime dependencies beyond the C++23 standard
 library, plus a pkg-config file so downstream projects can locate it with
 pkg-config --cflags djinni.
 .
 There is nothing to link against: everything Djinni provides is templates
 and inline functions in the installed headers.
